      "case": "two-events-from-one-source-have-two-effects",
      "deliveries": [
        {
          "source": "/invoicing",
          "id": "01923e8a-0000-7000-8000-000000000001"
        },
        {
          "source": "/invoicing",
          "id": "01923e8a-0000-7000-8000-000000000002"
        }
      ],
      "expect": {
        "effects": 2
      },
      "why": "AM3. Distinct ids are distinct events."
    },
    {
      "case": "the-same-id-from-two-sources-is-two-events",
      "deliveries": [
        {
          "source": "/invoicing",
          "id": "01923e8a-0000-7000-8000-000000000001"
        },
        {
          "source": "/billing",
          "id": "01923e8a-0000-7000-8000-000000000001"
        }
      ],
      "expect": {
        "effects": 2
      },
      "why": "AM3, and a VERIFIED DETECTOR. The key is (source, id), which is the identity CloudEvents guarantees unique; a consumer that deduplicates on id alone treats two producers' events as one and drops the second. This case fails such a consumer by name."
    },
    {
      "case": "a-failed-attempt-then-a-redelivery-has-one-effect",
      "deliveries": [
        {
          "source": "/invoicing",
          "id": "01923e8a-0000-7000-8000-000000000003",
          "fails": true
        },
        {
          "source": "/invoicing",
          "id": "01923e8a-0000-7000-8000-000000000003"
        }
      ],
      "expect": {
        "effects": 1
      },
      "why": "AM3 and AM5. The first attempt fails before the inbox row and the effect commit - they are one transaction - so nothing is recorded and the redelivery does the work once."
    },
    {
      "case": "a-dead-letter-replay-has-one-effect",
